Real code:

public Ticket parkVehicle(IVehicle vehicle){
    ISpot spot = spotFinder.findAvailableSpot(vehicle);
    spot.assign();

    Ticket ticket = new Ticket(vehicle, spot, System.currentTimeMillis());
    ticketManager.save(ticket);

    return ticket;
}

ParkingFacade.parkVehicle() alone touches SpotFinder, ISpot, Ticket, and TicketManager. Main.java only ever calls facade.parkVehicle(vehicle). What problem does ParkingFacade actually solve?,Nothing would break structurally if it didn't exist — it's purely cosmetic,SpotFinder and TicketManager would fail to compile without a Facade wrapping them,"Without it, the caller (Main) would have to know about and directly orchestrate SpotFinder, TicketManager, and each spot's assign()/release() itself — Facade centralizes that multi-collaborator coordination behind one entry point",It's required because IVehicle is declared as an interface rather than a class,,3,Facade's whole job is hiding multi-collaborator orchestration behind a single simple entry point so the caller never needs to know the internal wiring.,1

Real code:

public abstract class CommonSpot implements ISpot {
    protected IState state = AvailableState.getInstance();

    public void release() { state.release(this); }
    public void assign() { state.assign(this); }
}

instead of a simple private boolean isOccupied field with if-checks directly inside assign()/release(). What does the State pattern give you here that the boolean flag wouldn't?,"Each state (AvailableState, OccupiedState) owns its own transition rules in one place — adding a third state later (e.g. UNDER_MAINTENANCE) means writing one new IState class, not editing an if-else buried inside CommonSpot itself",No real benefit for just 2 states — a boolean flag would work identically with equivalent complexity,State pattern is required because Java doesn't allow boolean fields inside abstract classes,State pattern makes CommonSpot thread-safe automatically,,1,State lets each transition rule live in its own class instead of being edited inside a shared if-else — the real payoff shows up once a third state is added.,2

Real code:

public ParkingFacade(){
    this.ticketManager = new TicketManager();
    this.pricingStrategyFactory = new PricingStrategyFactory();
    this.spotFinder = new SpotFinder();
}

ParkingFacade constructs its own collaborators internally instead of receiving them as constructor parameters. What's the tradeoff being made here?,"This is strictly better than constructor injection in every case, with no downside",This pattern is required because SpotFinder has a private constructor,"Simplicity for the caller (Main.java just calls new ParkingFacade(), no wiring needed) at the cost of testability and flexibility — you can't substitute a fake SpotFinder or TicketManager in a unit test without editing ParkingFacade's own source","It has no effect on testability, only on runtime performance",,3,Building your own collaborators trades away substitutability for convenience — a real cost when you later want to unit-test ParkingFacade in isolation.,2

Real code:

public ISpot findAvailableSpot(IVehicle vehicle){
    ParkingLot lot = ParkingLot.getInstance();
    ...
}

SpotFinder reaches into the ParkingLot singleton directly instead of receiving a ParkingLot (or its floors) via its own constructor. What's lost by doing it this way?,Nothing is lost — ParkingLot must always be a singleton by definition of the domain,SpotFinder would fail to compile without calling ParkingLot.getInstance() directly,"This has no bearing on testing, it only affects thread safety","You lose the ability to test SpotFinder in isolation with a fake/smaller lot, its real dependency is hidden from anyone reading just its constructor, and it rules out ever running two independent ParkingLot instances (e.g. two branches) in the same process",,4,Reaching into a static singleton instead of accepting a dependency via the constructor hides what a class actually needs and blocks substituting a test double.,2

