static {
    strategies.put(SpotSize.COMPACT, new CompactSpot());
    strategies.put(SpotSize.LARGE, new LargeSpot());
    strategies.put(SpotSize.HEAVY, new HeavySpot());
}
public ISpot getSpot(SpotSize spotSize) {
    return strategies.get(spotSize);
}

and Main.java calls groundFloor.addSpot(spotFactory.getSpot(SpotSize.COMPACT)) twice in a row. How many distinct CompactSpot objects does groundFloor actually end up holding?,"Two distinct CompactSpot objects, exactly as expected","Just ONE — both calls return the SAME cached instance from the static map, so the floor's list holds two references to one object

Real code:

// AvailableState
public void release(ISpot spot) {
    System.out.println("cant release , is already available");
}

If ParkingFacade.unPark(ticket) is (accidentally) called twice on the same ticket, trace exactly what happens on the second call.,The second call throws an IllegalStateException immediately since the spot is already available,The second call returns 0.0 automatically since the ticket was already used once,The second call throws a NullPointerException since exitTime was already set,"ticket.setExitTime() overwrites exitTime with a new, later timestamp, the pricing strategy recalculates and returns a (possibly nonzero) fee again, and spot.release() silently prints a message and does nothing — nothing in unPark() can tell any of this went wrong, so the customer gets charged twice for one parking session",,4,"Nothing in this code path throws on an invalid second release — the failure is entirely silent, which is exactly what makes it dangerous.",3

Real code:

public List<Ticket> getAllTicketHistory() {
    // proxy pattern situation if this method is slow
    return allTickets;
}

Setting the performance comment aside for a moment — what's a plain encapsulation problem with this method as written?,"It returns the actual internal list by reference — any caller can call .add(), .remove(), or .clear() on the returned list and directly corrupt TicketManager's internal history, with no way for TicketManager to prevent or even notice it",It has no encapsulation problem at all — returning a List is always safe in Java,The only problem is that List is declared as an interface rather than a concrete ArrayList,It exposes a security vulnerability because Ticket contains payment information,,1,Returning the live reference means the caller effectively gets write access to private internal state through a getter that looks read-only.,1

Real code:

strategies.put(SpotSize.COMPACT, new HourlyBikePricingStrategy());
strategies.put(SpotSize.LARGE, new HourlyCarPricingStrategy());
strategies.put(SpotSize.HEAVY, new HourlyCarPricingStrategy());
...
public IPricingStrategy getPricingStrategy(IVehicle vehicle) {
    return strategies.get(vehicle.getSize());
}

Pricing is looked up by SpotSize, not by the vehicle's own type — today a clean 1:1 mapping. What future vehicle type would break this design?,"No future vehicle type could ever break this, since SpotSize only has 3 values",A vehicle that returns SpotSize.COMPACT would break compilation entirely,This design only fails if two vehicles have the exact same SpotSize AND identical pricing needs,"A LuxuryCar that also returns SpotSize.LARGE (fits the same spot as a regular FourWheeler) but needs its own different pricing strategy — since pricing is keyed by SpotSize, it would silently get the same HourlyCarPricingStrategy as an ordinary FourWheeler, with no way to tell them apart",,4,"Keying pricing off spot size instead of vehicle identity works only as long as ""one spot size = one pricing rule"" holds, and that assumption is fragile.",2
